  "query_preview": "..Very strong high pressure remains centered over \nArizona, with little movement expected over the next couple of days. \nDrier air has continued to find its way into the region but the \nmoisture isn't fully scoured out. As a result, we will continue to \nsee isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms across the \nhigher terrain, mainly focused over eastern Arizona through Monday. \nStorm activity will remain diurnally driven with showers and storms \ndwindling in coverage as we head into the evening each day. \n\nHeat remains the main story of the next couple of days with near to \nrecord temperatures across parts of the area. Extreme Heat Warnings \nand Heat Advisories remain in place through Monday with the Grand \nCanyon in effect through Tuesday. Lower elevations of the Grand \nCanyon may see the heat continue into Wednesday but with some \nuncertainty as to the placement of the high, we have held off on any \nadditional extensions. \n\nThe high elongates and slightly weakens but it remains strong enough \nto suppress any approaching troughs through the week. Moisture looks \nto gradually increase across the region for the first half of the \nweek as the high remains over Arizona, allowing for daily chances of \nisolated to scattered showers and storms each day, especially across \nthe higher terrain. Heading into midweek, the high starts to shift \nto the north. There remains some uncertainty as to where the high \nends up, with some models shifting the high directly north and \nothers making a slight westerly shift. Each solution comes with some \ndifferent impacts for the region. A more westerly shift would mean \ndrier and hotter conditions, while a more northerly shift would \nsignal increasing moisture, cooler temperatures, and potential for \nthunderstorms each day. Trends continue to increase moisture across \nthe region lasting into next weekend. In the end, the position of \nthe high will dictate exactly what happens across our area. Stay \ntuned as the scenario unfolds in the coming days!",
